#---
# purpose: "A tuple position that holds a list is a question too, so nested list shapes route the call"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.138. The tuple test carries one sub-predicate per position
# (fz-kdt.119) and a list test asks a cons cell its head (fz-kdt.107 step 3),
# but a position that could hold a LIST was excluded from both lowerings and
# from the lattice: `lowering_tests_position` answered "no" for it, so the
# position was blind, counted as overlapping and counted as erasing. The
# clause written first therefore took every value, exactly as the tag defect
# `dispatch_annotated_tuple_tag_clauses` pins and the element defect
# `dispatch_list_head_separates` pins, one nesting level in.
#
# Measured at 81ec3f25d, on `interp`, `run` and `build` alike: `kind` printed
# `1 1`, `dnik` printed `2 2`, `axis` printed `1 1`, `size` printed `1 1`,
# `deep` printed `1 1` and `peed` printed `2 2` -- six of the seven two-clause
# functions answered by clause order rather than by the value. `sixa` was
# right by accident: a blind position only ever ACCEPTS, so the clause whose
# list-free position still asks something (`{:a, integer}`) filters correctly
# when it is written first, and only the list-first order shows the defect.
# That is why each disjoint pair is written both ways round here.
#
# The position is asked now, so each pair below is two questions.
#
# ## The five shapes a nested list position has to separate
#
#     kind/dnik   disjoint HEADS       {:a, [integer]} vs {:a, [:ok | :err]}
#     axis/sixa   list vs NON-list     {:a, [integer]} vs {:a, integer}
#     size        the EMPTY shape      {:a, []}        vs {:a, [integer]}
#     deep/peed   through a TUPLE      {:a, {:b, [integer]}} vs {:a, {:b, [:ok | :err]}}
#     twin/niwt   TWO list positions   {[integer], [:ok | :err]} vs {[:ok | :err], [integer]}
#
# The `pick_*` helpers are what make each one a RUNTIME question. Called with
# a literal instead, the argument's own type settles the clause at compile
# time and every shape above answers correctly even blind -- measured at
# 81ec3f25d, `1 2 1 2 1 2` for the same three pairs -- which is why a
# literal-argument probe reports no defect at all. The value has to be one the
# compiler cannot fold.
#
# ## Why `size` is written in one order and the others in two
#
# A list type is `[]` joined with the lists over its element type, so
# `{:a, []}` is CONTAINED IN `{:a, [integer]}` -- the pair is nested, not
# disjoint, and seating the wide one first legitimately answers `2` for both
# values (Elixir answers the same for the same clause order). Only the
# narrow-first order asks a question the blind position could get wrong, and
# that is the order written here. The other three pairs are disjoint, so both
# their orders print the same two lines, which is the claim those twins carry.
fn kind(x :: {:a, [integer]}), do: 1
fn kind(x :: {:a, [:ok | :err]}), do: 2

fn dnik(x :: {:a, [:ok | :err]}), do: 2
fn dnik(x :: {:a, [integer]}), do: 1

fn axis(x :: {:a, [integer]}), do: 1
fn axis(x :: {:a, integer}), do: 2

fn sixa(x :: {:a, integer}), do: 2
fn sixa(x :: {:a, [integer]}), do: 1

fn size(x :: {:a, []}), do: 1
fn size(x :: {:a, [integer]}), do: 2

fn deep(x :: {:a, {:b, [integer]}}), do: 1
fn deep(x :: {:a, {:b, [:ok | :err]}}), do: 2

fn peed(x :: {:a, {:b, [:ok | :err]}}), do: 2
fn peed(x :: {:a, {:b, [integer]}}), do: 1

fn twin(x :: {[integer], [:ok | :err]}), do: 1
fn twin(x :: {[:ok | :err], [integer]}), do: 2

fn niwt(x :: {[:ok | :err], [integer]}), do: 2
fn niwt(x :: {[integer], [:ok | :err]}), do: 1

fn pick_head(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    {:a, [n, n]}
  else
    {:a, [:ok, :err]}
  end
end

fn pick_axis(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    {:a, [n, n]}
  else
    {:a, n}
  end
end

fn pick_size(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    {:a, []}
  else
    {:a, [n]}
  end
end

fn pick_deep(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    {:a, {:b, [n, n]}}
  else
    {:a, {:b, [:ok, :err]}}
  end
end

fn pick_twin(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    {[n, n], [:ok, :err]}
  else
    {[:ok, :err], [n, n]}
  end
end

fn main() do
  dbg(kind(pick_head(1)))
  dbg(kind(pick_head(0)))
  dbg(dnik(pick_head(1)))
  dbg(dnik(pick_head(0)))

  dbg(axis(pick_axis(1)))
  dbg(axis(pick_axis(0)))
  dbg(sixa(pick_axis(1)))
  dbg(sixa(pick_axis(0)))

  dbg(size(pick_size(1)))
  dbg(size(pick_size(0)))

  dbg(deep(pick_deep(1)))
  dbg(deep(pick_deep(0)))
  dbg(peed(pick_deep(1)))
  dbg(peed(pick_deep(0)))

  dbg(twin(pick_twin(1)))
  dbg(twin(pick_twin(0)))
  dbg(niwt(pick_twin(1)))
  dbg(niwt(pick_twin(0)))
end
